<headroom-library version="1" id="clean-hollowbody-bebop" category="Clean" order="70" instrument="guitar" style="Jazz">
  <description>The late-50s/60s studio jazz sound: a hollowbody into the normal channel of a small tweed combo, tone low, just loud enough that hard-picked lines thicken while comping stays clean. Warm, woody and a little compressed by the amp itself, in a small live room.</description>
  <tags><tag>Clean</tag><tag>Jazz</tag><tag>Tweed</tag><tag>Warm</tag><tag>Room</tag></tags>
  <sources>
    <source url="https://www.organissimo.org/forum/topic/32537-what-kind-of-guitar-amp-did-rudy-van-gelder-have-in-the-60s/" title="Organissimo forum: Rudy Van Gelder's studio guitar amp in the 60s"/>
    <source url="https://www.jazzguitar.be/forum/players/34066-grant-green-kenny-burrell-sound.html" title="jazzguitar.be forum: Grant Green / Kenny Burrell sound"/>
    <source url="https://www.jazzguitarlessons.net/blog/kenny-burrell" title="JazzGuitarLessons.net: Kenny Burrell"/>
    <source url="https://www.jazzguitar.be/blog/jazz-guitarists-guitar-gear/" title="jazzguitar.be: famous jazz guitarists and their gear"/>
  </sources>
</headroom-library>
